{"number": 146, "text": "                    Unit  33\n                    1 fall              5  making\n\n                    2 distinction       6  case\n                    3 take              7  touch\n                    4 raise\n                    1 assess            6  hold\n           33.2\n                    2 convincingly      7  draw\n                    3 draw              8  lay\n                    4  lend              9 profoundly\n                    5  forward          10 draw\n                    1  presents          5 briefly\n\n                    2  suggests          6 draws\n                    3  adopts            7 hold\n                    4  states            8 raises\n                    Kerr takes up a controversial position in his latest article. He puts forward the argument that\n            33.4\n                    differences in behaviour between the sexes can be explained totally by the genes. He attempts to\n                    make  a case for educating boys and girls separately in their primary school years. He argues,\n                    occasionally convincingly, that both sexes would benefit from this. He draws attention to recent\n                     research which, he claims, lends support to his argument. However, he fails to take a number of\n                     important factors into consideration. He also makes no reference to the important work of Potter\n                     and Sinclair in this field. | am sure that | will not be alone in disagreeing profoundly with many of his\n                     conclusions.\n                     Over to you\n                     Possible collocations are:\n                     revolutionary theory, dismiss/disprove someone’s theory\n                     groundbreaking  research, a major research project\n                     present one’s argument, a powerful argument\n\n                     Unit  34\n                        obey/observe     6  passing/introducing\n            34.1\n\n                        observe/obey     7  introducing/passing\n                        break            8  upholding/enforcing\n                        respect          9  enforcing/upholding\n                        act\n                        The rules apply to all students in the college.\n\n             34.\n                        The rules prohibit the use of mobile phones in class.\n                        The rules allow students to book college guestrooms at weekends.\n                        The regulations stipulate that coursework must be handed in on time.\n                        Most students follow the rules without too many complaints.\n                        The authorities bent the rules to allow Mary to submit her coursework a little late.\n                        All students must comply with the regulations.\n                        e) Bill Sikes robs a bank.\n             34.3\n                        g) The police carry out an investigation.\n                        b) Bill Sikes goes on trial.\n                        a)   Anumber of witnesses appear in court.\n                        f) The jury reaches its verdict.\n                        c) Bill Sikes is found guilty.\n                        d) Bill Sikes is severely punished.\n                        to win his or her case\n                         a fair trial\n                         carry out an investigation\n                         They may face the death penalty.\n\n                      English Collocations in Use Intermediate\n       144", "char_count": 3481, "mean_conf": 92.94, "word_count": 359, "words": [{"text": "144", "x0": 148, "y0": 3348, "x1": 209, "y1": 3374, "conf": 95, "bold": true}, {"text": "3301", "x0": 224, "y0": 333, "x1": 324, "y1": 369, "conf": 33, "bold": false}, {"text": "33.2", "x0": 227, "y0": 578, "x1": 326, "y1": 614, "conf": 86, "bold": false}, {"text": "RC", "x0": 230, "y0": 876, "x1": 331, "y1": 913, "conf": 19, "bold": false}, {"tex